### Implement Platform-Specific Code with Preprocessor Directives

Source: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/test-conditional-compilation.html

Demonstrates the use of C# preprocessor directives to log messages to the console based on the active build target. This allows developers to isolate platform-specific logic within a single script.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using System.Collections;

public class PlatformDefines : MonoBehaviour {
	void Start () {

		#if UNITY_EDITOR
			Debug.Log("Unity Editor");
		#endif

		#if UNITY_IOS
			Debug.Log("Unity iOS");
		#endif

		#if UNITY_STANDALONE_OSX
			Debug.Log("Standalone OSX");
		#endif

		#if UNITY_STANDALONE_WIN
			Debug.Log("Standalone Windows");
		#endif
	}
}
```

### Conditional compilation in Unity > Unity scripting symbol reference > Unity Editor version symbols

Source: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/scripting-symbol-reference.html

Unity automatically exposes global scripting symbols based on the version of the Unity Editor currently in use. These symbols follow the format UNITY_X, UNITY_X_Y, and UNITY_X_Y_Z, which correspond to the major, minor, and patch release numbers of the Editor. This system allows developers to write code that is compatible with multiple versions of Unity while selectively enabling features that are only available in newer releases. Additionally, Unity provides the UNITY_X_Y_OR_NEWER directive, which is particularly useful for ensuring that specific code sections only compile when using a minimum required version of the engine.

--------------------------------

### Referencing assemblies > Defining symbols based on Unity and project package versions

Source: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/assembly-definition-includes.html

If you need to compile different code in an assembly according to whether a project uses specific versions of Unity or of a package, you can add entries to the **Version Defines** list. This list specifies rules for when a symbol should be defined. For version numbers, you can specify a logical expression that evaluates to a specific version or a range of versions. To conditionally define a symbol, select the Assembly Definition asset, then in the **Version Defines** section, click **+** to add an entry. Set the **If resource** (Unity or a package/module), the **version is** (an expression for version or range), and the **set define** (the scripting symbol to define).

